Step 1: Assessment and Tool-Based Auditing

Before altering code, identify potential execution blockages, internal runtime dependencies, and unsupported APIs.
• Analyze Dependencies: Execute mvn dependency:tree or ./gradlew dependencies to log every package.
• Detect Internal APIs: Run jdeps --jdk-internals --multi-release 21 your-application.jar to flag dependencies using non-public sun.* or jdk.internal.* packages.
• Scan for Removed Code: Use the JDK jdeprscan tool (jdeprscan --release 21 your-application.jar) to find elements flagged for removal that will cause compile errors.

Step 2: Incremental Upgrades (The LTS Ladder)

Do not attempt a "big-bang" jump directly into Java 21 production. Instead, step sequentially through Long-Term Support (LTS) versions to isolate errors.
[ Java 8 ] ──> [ Java 11 ] ──> [ Java 17 ] ──> [ Java 21 ]
• Java 8 → 11: Expect build breaks from removed Java EE modules like JAXB (javax.xml.bind), JAX-WS, and CORBA. Resolve these by adding them as explicit third-party dependencies. Java 9's Module System (JPMS) will also begin enforcing early library boundaries.
• Java 11 → 17: Stricter encapsulation blocks reflective hacks into internal JDK structures. Upgrading core bytecode frameworks (like Lombok, ByteBuddy, or ASM) is mandatory here.
• Java 17 → 21: This is the smoothest stage, as the surrounding ecosystem is already modern-runtime compliant.

Step 3: Modernize Infrastructure & Frameworks

Your build pipelines and foundational frameworks must explicitly support the Java 21 platform.
• Upgrade Build Tools: Update Maven (to 3.9+) or Gradle (to 8.5+) alongside corresponding compiler and testing plugins.
• Migrate to Jakarta EE: Enterprise frameworks require transitioning namespaces. For example, Spring Boot 3.x mandates Java 17/21 and moves completely from javax.* to jakarta.* packages.
• Automate via OpenRewrite: For large codebases, look into automation recipes like OpenRewrite to auto-correct standard API shifts, update pom.xml declarations, and swap deprecated patterns cleanly.

Step 4: JVM Flag Cleanup and Runtime Testing

The underlying garbage collection architectures and localized behaviors changed entirely between these versions.
• Purge Deprecated Flags: Remove deprecated JVM garbage collection settings—such as the Concurrent Mark Sweep (CMS) options, which were entirely removed in Java 14.
• Audit Locale Defaults: Keep in mind that JDK 9+ defaults to CLDR locale data. If your system relies on legacy Java 8 date/currency string formatting, you may need the fallback flag -Djava.locale.providers=COMPAT,CLDR.
• Encoding Changes: Java 18 switched the default charset to UTF-8. Ensure code expecting the host OS default encoding handles file I/O operations correctly.

Step 5: Runtime Profiling & Optimization

Once compilation and automated integration testing pass, leverage Java 21's capabilities.
• Adopt New Language Features: Safely refactor boilerplate code using modern features like Records, Sealed Classes, Pattern Matching for switch, and Sequenced Collections.
• Introduce Virtual Threads: For heavy synchronous I/O or servlet-based architectures, exchange resource-intensive platform threads for high-throughput Virtual Threads (Project Loom) to drastically lower hardware overhead.

