 Autoboxing and Unboxing Mechanisms

Because Java collections (like ArrayList) require objects and cannot store raw primitives, Java introduces Wrapper Classes (Integer, Double, etc.). The automatic conversion between these two worlds is known as Autoboxing and Unboxing.
• Autoboxing: The automatic conversion of a primitive type into its corresponding wrapper object.
• Unboxing: The automatic conversion of a wrapper object back into its raw primitive value.

Under the Hood

The compiler automatically injects specific static methods to handle these transitions:
java
// --- What you write ---
Integer wrapperInt = 42;          // Autoboxing
int primitiveInt = wrapperInt;    // Unboxing

// --- What the compiler actually executes ---
Integer wrapperInt = Integer.valueOf(42); 
int primitiveInt = wrapperInt.intValue();

###############


The Performance Trap: Integer Caching & Overhead

• The Cache: To save memory, Java caches Integer objects for values from -128 to 127. Integer.valueOf(42) returns a pre-existing cached object.
• The Trap: Going outside this range (e.g., Integer.valueOf(1000)) forces Java to instantiate a brand-new object on the heap every single time.
• Silent Performance Bleed: If you perform arithmetic inside a loop using wrapper objects, Java silently box/unbox cycles on every iteration, polluting the heap and triggering aggressive Garbage Collection (GC).
java
// Terrible Performance Example
Integer sum = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < 1000000; i++) {
    sum += i; // Unboxes sum, adds i, boxes results into a NEW Integer object!
}

