Overhauling Generics to Eliminate Wrapper Classes

Java historically relied on Type Erasure. Generic types like List<T> compile down to List<Object> at runtime. Because primitives cannot be assigned to Object, Java forced the use of heap-allocated wrapper companions (Integer, Double).
To support declarations like List<int> or List<Point> directly, Valhalla transforms the JVM's type system through a multi-step redesign:
Legacy Type System (Erasure):
List<Integer> ──[Compiles To]──> List<Object> ──[Stores]──> Pointers to Heap Boxes

Valhalla Universal Model (Specialization):
List<int>     ──[Parametric JVM]─> List${type=int} ──[Stores]──> Flat, primitive byte slots

1. Universal Generics & Unifying Top Types

In classic Java, Object is the top type solely for identity classes. Valhalla broadens the type hierarchy so that primitives, value types, and traditional reference types all share a single conceptual root. This allows generic type parameters (<T>) to range over all JVM types, not merely references.

2. Generic Specialization (The Parametric JVM)

Rather than erasing every type argument to Object, the JVM compiles generic classes into templates capable of specializing at runtime:
• Reified Bytecode on Demand: When code requests ArrayList<int>, the JVM instantiates a specialized runtime template backed directly by int[] instead of Object[].
• No Box-Unbox Instruction Cycles: Operations like list.get(i) retrieve raw primitive bits directly from contiguous memory instead of invoking synthetic conversions like Integer.valueOf() and .intValue().
• Zero Pointer Chasing: Memory cache misses drop sharply because values sit in adjacent bytes rather than scattered reference locations.

3. Null-Restricted Types (T! vs. T?)

Primitives cannot be null, while reference classes can. To prevent specialized collections from breaking null safety, Valhalla introduces null-restricted types:
• Point! / int: Guarantees the value cannot be null. The JVM can flatten the storage entirely down to payload bytes without allocating an auxiliary null-marker pointer.
• Point?: Preserves nullability when an empty state is required, using a reference or companion bit to track whether the element is present.

