Java Generics allow you to parameterize types (classes, interfaces, and methods). This enables you to reuse code with different data types while enforcing compile-time type safety and eliminating the need for manual type casting.
Before generics were introduced in Java 5, collections stored raw Object references, forcing developers to manually cast objects and risking runtime errors like ClassCastException.

1. Generic Classes

A generic class is declared with a type parameter section—specified inside angle brackets (<>)—following the class name.
java
// 'T' is a type parameter acting as a placeholder for the actual type passed later
public class Box<T> {
    private T content;

    public void setContent(T content) {
        this.content = content;
    }

    public T getContent() {
        return content;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Instantiate a Box specifically for Strings
        Box<String> stringBox = new Box<>();
        stringBox.setContent("Hello Generics");
        String str = stringBox.getContent(); // No manual casting needed!

        // Instantiate the same Box class for Integers
        Box<Integer> intBox = new Box<>();
        intBox.setContent(123);
        
        // Compile-time safety error check:
        // intBox.setContent("Wrong Type"); // Compile-time Error! Prevents bugs early.
    }
}
Use code with caution.

2. Generic Methods

You can also make individual methods generic, even if the surrounding class is not. The type parameter is placed right before the method's return type.
java
public class MethodUtility {

    // Generic method that accepts an array of any type T and prints its contents
    public static <T> void printArray(T[] array) {
        for (T element : array) {
            System.out.print(element + " ");
        }
        System.out.println();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Integer[] intArray = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
        String[] stringArray = {"Java", "Generics", "Tutorial"};

        // The compiler automatically infers the type of T at runtime
        printArray(intArray);    // Outputs: 1 2 3 4 5 
        printArray(stringArray); // Outputs: Java Generics Tutorial 
    }
}
Use code with caution.

3. Bounded Type Parameters (Restricting Types)

Sometimes you want to limit the types that can be passed to a generic class or method. Bounded types allow you to restrict the type parameter using the extends keyword.
java
public class NumericStats<T extends Number> {
    private T[] numbers;

    public NumericStats(T[] numbers) {
        this.numbers = numbers;
    }

    public double getAverage() {
        double sum = 0.0;
        for (T num : numbers) {
            // Safe to call doubleValue() because T must be a subclass of Number
            sum += num.doubleValue(); 
        }
        return sum / numbers.length;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Integer[] intArray = {1, 2, 3};
        NumericStats<Integer> intStats = new NumericStats<>(intArray);
        System.out.println(intStats.getAverage()); // Outputs: 2.0

        // This will cause a COMPILE ERROR because String does not extend Number:
        // String[] strArray = {"A", "B"};
        // NumericStats<String> strStats = new NumericStats<>(strArray); 
    }
}
Use code with caution.

4. Wildcards (?) in Generics

Wildcards are used when you want a generic argument to represent an unknown type. They come in three variations:

A. Unbounded Wildcard (<?>)

Represents any type. Useful when a method relies on functionality provided by the base Object class.
java
public static void printList(List<?> list) {
    for (Object elem : list) {
        System.out.println(elem);
    }
}
Use code with caution.

B. Upper-Bounded Wildcard (<? extends T>)

Restricts the unknown type to a specific type or its subclasses. This is useful when you want to read data from a structure.
java
// Accepts a list of Numbers, Integers, Doubles, etc.
public static double sumOfList(List<? extends Number> list) {
    double sum = 0.0;
    for (Number n : list) {
        sum += n.doubleValue();
    }
    return sum;
}
Use code with caution.

C. Lower-Bounded Wildcard (<? super T>)

Restricts the unknown type to a specific type or its superclasses. This is useful when you want to write or add data to a structure.
java
// Accepts a list of Integer, Number, or Object
public static void addNumbers(List<? super Integer> list) {
    list.add(1);
    list.add(2);
}
Use code with caution.

Standard Naming Conventions

To keep your code readable, Java developers use standard single-letter type parameter names:
• T - Type (General placeholder)
• E - Element (Used extensively by the Java Collections Framework, e.g., List<E>)
• K - Key (Used in Maps, e.g., Map<K, V>)
• V - Value (Used in Maps)
• N - Number


